# The tutor for this Neovim config

This is a guided tour of *your* setup: the keys, pickers and workflows this
config adds on top of plain Neovim. For Vim itself, see
[the stock tutor](@tutor:vim-01-beginner).

How to use it:

- This buffer is a scratch copy. Edit it freely, nothing here is ever saved.
- A line inside a command block can be run from here: put the cursor on it
  and press `>>`{normal}.
- `<CR>`{normal} on a link opens it, e.g. [the quickfix help](quickfix).
- A ✗ in the sign column marks an exercise line. It becomes ✓ when the line
  is right.
- Leader is `<Space>`{normal}. Press it and wait: which-key lists every key
  that can follow. That popup is the index of everything taught here.

Lessons (press `<CR>`{normal} on one to jump there):

 0. [Set up the sandbox](*setup*)
 1. [Editing primitives](*editing*)              surround, text objects
 2. [Moving around](*moving*)                   windows, buffers, files
 3. [Finding things](*finding*)                  pickers
 4. [Editing by structure](*structure*)            treesitter
 5. [The language server](*server*)             navigate, rename, complete
 6. [Diagnostics](*diagnostics*)                     read, jump, fix
 7. [Copying context](*copying*)                 the Space-y family
 8. [Quickfix](*quickfix*)                        one list for everything
 9. [Tests and builds](*tests*)                failures you can jump to
10. [Formatting and linting](*formatting*)          what runs on save, and when
11. [jj and reviewing changes](*changes*)        hunks, agent review, split
12. [The terminal](*terminal*)
13. [Messages, sessions, toggles](*messages*)
14. [Daily drill and cheat sheet](*drill*)

Do one or two lessons a day and repeat the daily drill (lesson 14). Keys
become muscle memory through repetition, not through reading.

# Lesson 0: SET UP THE SANDBOX *setup*

Most lessons are practised in a throwaway project with real language
servers, a failing test and a jj repository. Create it and open it in a
second tab by pressing `>>`{normal} on the next line:

~~~ cmd
TutorSandbox
~~~

- `gt`{normal} and `gT`{normal} switch between this tutor and the sandbox tab.
- The sandbox tab has its own working directory, so pickers, tests and jj
  all act on the sandbox, never on a real project.
- Broke something? `:TutorSandbox!`{vim} throws it away and starts clean.

Come back here with `gT`{normal} now.

# Lesson 1: EDITING PRIMITIVES *editing*

Practise these right here. Each line shows ✗ until it matches the goal.

## Surround: `sa`{normal} add, `sd`{normal} delete, `sr`{normal} replace

 1. Put the cursor on the word editor and type `saiw"`{normal}
    (surround-add, inner word, with a double quote).

Wrap the word editor in double quotes.

 2. Put the cursor inside the parentheses and type `sd)`{normal}.

Take the (parentheses) away from this line.

 3. Put the cursor inside the quotes and type `sr"'`{normal}
    (surround-replace: from double quote to single quote).

Turn "double quotes" into single quotes.

 4. Select `a + b` with `v`{normal}, then type `saf`{normal}, enter the
    name wrap and press `<CR>`{normal}. The `f` surrounding is a function
    call.

Call wrap on this expression: a + b

## Text objects: `i`{normal} inside, `a`{normal} around

mini.ai adds `q` for any quote and `b` for any bracket, and it searches
forward, so the cursor only has to be on the line, before the target.

 5. From the start of the line type `ciq`{normal}, then new text, then
    `<Esc>`{normal}. Goal: translate("new text")

greeting = translate("old text here")

 6. From the start of the line type `dib`{normal}. Goal: values = []

values = [first, second, third]

More of the same family, try them on the lines above:

- `ci(`{normal} `da"`{normal} `yi{`{normal}    the usual objects, now multi-line aware
- `ciNq`{normal} `ciLq`{normal}          the *next* / *last* quote, skipping the current
- `g[`{normal} `g]`{normal}              jump to the left / right edge of an object
- `vii`{normal} `vai`{normal}            the current indent scope (the guide line)
- `[i`{normal} `]i`{normal}              jump to the top / bottom of that scope

## Small things worth knowing

- Brackets and quotes close themselves as you type (mini.pairs).
- `gcc`{normal} comments a line, `gc`{normal} plus a motion a region, e.g.
  `gcip`{normal}.
- `[<Space>`{normal} and `]<Space>`{normal} add a blank line above / below.
- In visual mode `p`{normal} pastes over the selection *without* replacing
  what you yanked, so you can paste the same thing repeatedly.
- `<Esc>`{normal} clears search highlighting.
- `n`{normal} `N`{normal} `<C-d>`{normal} `<C-u>`{normal} keep the cursor
  line centred.

# Lesson 2: MOVING AROUND *moving*

## Windows and tmux panes

`<C-h>`{normal} `<C-j>`{normal} `<C-k>`{normal} `<C-l>`{normal} move between
Neovim windows and continue into the neighbouring tmux pane at the edge. The
same keys work everywhere, including the file explorer.

 1. In the sandbox tab type `:vsplit`{vim}, then move left and right with
    `<C-h>`{normal} and `<C-l>`{normal}. Close one with `:q`{vim}.
 2. `<C-Up>`{normal} `<C-Down>`{normal} `<C-Left>`{normal}
    `<C-Right>`{normal} resize the current window.

## Buffers

 3. Open two files in the sandbox, then switch with `]b`{normal} and
    `[b`{normal}. `3]b`{normal} skips three. `[B`{normal} and `]B`{normal}
    go to the first and last.
 4. `<Space><Space>`{normal} lists open buffers in a picker.
 5. `<Space>bd`{normal} closes a buffer but keeps its window.

## Files: oil

 6. Press `-`{normal} in any file: its directory opens *as a buffer*.
    `<CR>`{normal} opens an entry, `-`{normal} goes up again.
 7. The listing is editable text. In the sandbox: go to a file name, change
    it with `cw`{normal}, then `:w`{vim}. Oil shows what it is about to do;
    confirm with `y`{normal} and the file is renamed. `dd`{normal} plus
    `:w`{vim} deletes, `o`{normal} plus a name plus `:w`{vim} creates.
    Rename it back when you are done.
 8. Inside oil: `<C-s>`{normal} opens in a vertical split, `<C-x>`{normal}
    in a horizontal one, `<C-p>`{normal} previews, `g.`{normal} shows hidden
    files, `gR`{normal} refreshes, `g?`{normal} lists all keys.

## Getting back

- `<C-o>`{normal} and `<C-i>`{normal} walk the jump list backward and
  forward. `<Space>sj`{normal} shows it in a picker.
- Reopening a file puts the cursor where you left it.

# Lesson 3: FINDING THINGS *finding*

Everything under `<Space>s`{normal} is a search. Do these in the sandbox tab.

 1. `<Space>sf`{normal} finds files. Type inv and press `<CR>`{normal}.
 2. `<Space>sg`{normal} greps as you type. Search for Total.
 3. Put the cursor on a word and press `<Space>sw`{normal}: grep for that
    word. In visual mode it greps for the selection.
 4. `<Space>/`{normal} fuzzy-searches the lines of the current buffer.
 5. `<Space>sr`{normal} reopens the last picker exactly where you left it.
    This is the one to build a habit around.
 6. `<Space>s.`{normal} recent files, `<Space>sn`{normal} this config's
    files, `<Space>sh`{normal} help, `<Space>sk`{normal} keymaps.
 7. `<Space>sS`{normal} lists every picker there is. Worth browsing once.

## Keys inside every picker

| Key         | Does                                               |
|-------------|----------------------------------------------------|
| <Tab>       | select several entries                             |
| alt-a       | select / deselect all                              |
| alt-q       | send the selection to the quickfix list            |
| ctrl-s      | open in a split (ctrl-v vertical, ctrl-t tab)      |
| ctrl-g      | in grep: switch between live grep and fuzzy refine |
| alt-i alt-h | include ignored / hidden files                     |
| F4          | toggle the preview (shift-down / up scroll it)     |
| F1          | show all keys of this picker                       |

 8. Run `<Space>sg`{normal}, type quantity, press `alt-a`{normal} then
    `alt-q`{normal}. The matches are now in the quickfix list. Step through
    them with `]q`{normal} and `[q`{normal}. More on that in
    lesson 8.

Also: `z=`{normal} on a misspelled word (with `<Space>ts`{normal} on) offers
spelling suggestions in a picker.

# Lesson 4: EDITING BY STRUCTURE *structure*

Treesitter knows the syntax tree, so you can select and move by function,
class and parameter instead of by character. Use `orders.py` in the sandbox.

## Grow and shrink a selection

 1. Put the cursor on `percent` inside `apply_discount`. Press `v`{normal},
    then `an`{normal} several times. The selection grows: name, expression,
    statement, function, class. `in`{normal} shrinks it again.
    This is the fastest way to select "the thing I am in".

## Functions, classes, parameters

| Object | Is        | Try         |
|--------|-----------|-------------|
| af if  | function  | vaf dif yaf |
| ac ic  | class     | vac         |
| aa ia  | parameter | cia daa     |

 2. Inside `summary`, type `vif`{normal}: the body. `<Esc>`{normal}, then
    `vaf`{normal}: the whole method.
 3. On the parameter `minimum` type `daa`{normal}: gone, comma included.
    Undo with `u`{normal}.

## Move by function and class

 4. `]m`{normal} and `[m`{normal} jump to the next / previous function
    start. `]M`{normal} `[M`{normal} to function ends.
 5. `]]`{normal} and `[[`{normal} jump between classes.

## Swap parameters

 6. On `percent` in the `apply_discount` signature press `<Space>a`{normal}:
    it trades places with the next parameter. `<Space>A`{normal} swaps it
    back.

## Know where you are

 7. Make the window short with `:split`{vim}, put the cursor in `summary`
    and scroll with `<C-e>`{normal} until the method's first line leaves the
    screen. The class and method lines stay pinned at the top (sticky
    context). `[c`{normal} jumps to that line, `<Space>tc`{normal} turns the
    feature off and on. Close the split with `:q`{vim}.
 8. Folds follow the syntax tree: `za`{normal} toggles the fold under the
    cursor, `zM`{normal} closes all, `zR`{normal} opens all.

# Lesson 5: THE LANGUAGE SERVER *server*

Stay in `orders.py`. The `gr` family is Neovim's own LSP prefix.

| Key | Does                                   |
|-----|----------------------------------------|
| K   | hover docs (press again to enter them) |
| gd  | definition                             |
| gD  | declaration                            |
| grr | references                             |
| gri | implementation                         |
| grt | type definition                        |
| grn | rename                                 |
| gra | code actions, with a diff preview      |
| gO  | outline of this file                   |

 1. On `Item(` in `main` press `gd`{normal}. You land on the class.
    `<C-o>`{normal} takes you back.
 2. On `total` (the method in `Item`) press `grr`{normal}: every use, in a
    picker with preview.
 3. On `subtotal` press `grn`{normal}, change the name, `<CR>`{normal}. Every
    reference is renamed. Undo with `u`{normal}, or keep it.
 4. `gO`{normal} shows the file's outline. `<Space>ss`{normal} searches
    symbols across the whole workspace as you type.
 5. `K`{normal} on `dataclass` shows its documentation in a bordered float.
 6. `<Space>th`{normal} toggles inlay hints (inferred types shown inline).

## Completion

 7. At the end of `main` start a new line and type `order.`: the menu opens.
    - `<C-n>`{normal} `<C-p>`{normal} move through it.
    - `<C-y>`{normal} accepts. `<CR>`{normal} never accepts, it is always a
      plain newline.
    - `<C-Space>`{normal} opens the menu or toggles the documentation.
    - `<C-e>`{normal} dismisses it.
    - After accepting a snippet, `<Tab>`{normal} and `<S-Tab>`{normal} jump
      between its placeholders.
    - Inside a call's parentheses the signature appears by itself.

If a server misbehaves: `<Space>lr`{normal} restarts it, `<Space>cl`{normal}
shows its health report.

# Lesson 6: DIAGNOSTICS *diagnostics*

`orders.py` has problems on purpose: two unused imports and two typos.
Signs in the gutter mark the lines: E is an error, W a warning.

## Read

 1. Move onto the line with `lable`. The error expands *below* the line with
    its full text. Warnings (try the `import os` line) appear at the end of
    the line instead, so the code does not jump around.
 2. `<C-w>d`{normal} opens everything for this line in a float, with source
    and code. Press it again to enter the float; `q`{normal} leaves.

## Jump

 3. `]d`{normal} and `[d`{normal} go to the next / previous diagnostic and
    centre it. `3]d`{normal} skips three. `]D`{normal} `[D`{normal} jump to
    the last and first in the buffer.
 4. `<Space>sd`{normal} lists this buffer's diagnostics in a picker,
    `<Space>sD`{normal} those of the workspace.

## Fix

 5. On `import os` press `gra`{normal}: Ruff offers to remove the unused
    import. Accept it. Then fix `lable` and `subtotl` by hand (or with
    `grn`{normal} if you like to live dangerously).

Each finding has one owner here: Ruff reports unused imports and variables
(it can fix them), basedpyright reports undefined names and all type errors.

# Lesson 7: COPYING CONTEXT *copying*

When you ask a colleague or an agent for help, they need the location, the
message and the code. The `<Space>y`{normal} family puts exactly that on the
system clipboard. Undo your fixes from lesson 6 first (`u`{normal}), or run
`:TutorSandbox!`{vim}, so there is something to copy.

 1. On the `lable` line press `<Space>yd`{normal}. Come back here
    (`gT`{normal}) and paste below with `p`{normal}:



    You get the path from the repository root, line, column, severity,
    source and rule, the full message, a documentation link and the line of
    code.
 2. Select a few lines with `V`{normal} and press `<Space>yd`{normal}: all
    diagnostics inside the selection.
 3. `<Space>yD`{normal} copies every diagnostic in the buffer,
    `<Space>yw`{normal} every one Neovim knows about.
 4. In the diagnostics picker (`<Space>sd`{normal}) mark entries with
    `<Tab>`{normal} and press `ctrl-y`{normal}: the full text of those, and
    the picker stays open.

Not only diagnostics:

| Key       | Copies                                                    |
|-----------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| <Space>yp | the file's path                                           |
| <Space>yl | path:line, or path:first-last for a selection             |
| <Space>yc | the code as a fenced block with its location and problems |

 5. Select the `format_price` function with `vaf`{normal}, press
    `<Space>yc`{normal} and paste here:



Use `<Space>yl`{normal} when the reader can open the file (an agent in the
same repo), `<Space>yc`{normal} when they cannot (a chat, an issue).

# Lesson 8: QUICKFIX *quickfix*

The quickfix list is one list of locations you step through. Greps,
diagnostics, changed hunks and test failures all land in it, and the same
two keys walk all of them: `]q`{normal} next, `[q`{normal} previous
(`[Q`{normal} `]Q`{normal} first and last).

 1. `<Space>qd`{normal} loads every diagnostic into the list.
    `<Space>qe`{normal} only the errors. Step through with `]q`{normal}.
 2. `<Space>qq`{normal} hides and shows the list window.
 3. Narrow it: `:Cfilter /imported/`{vim} keeps the entries whose text
    matches, `:Cfilter! /imported/`{vim} drops them instead.
 4. `:colder`{vim} and `:cnewer`{vim} go back and forth between the lists you
    have made. Nothing is lost when a new one arrives.
 5. `:grep quantity`{vim} fills the list from ripgrep without a picker.
 6. Change every hit at once: `:cdo s/quantity/count/ | update`{vim}.
    It edits and saves each file in the list; `:TutorSandbox!`{vim} resets
    the sandbox afterwards.
 7. `<Space>sq`{normal} fuzzy-searches the list itself.

`<Space>qw`{normal} asks the language servers to check the whole workspace,
not just open files. Not every server supports it; results arrive in the
background and `<Space>qd`{normal} shows them.

# Lesson 9: TESTS AND BUILDS *tests*

Tests run in the background and failures become quickfix entries.

 1. Open `inventory.go` in the sandbox and press `<Space>qt`{normal}
    (or `:Test`{vim}). You keep editing while it runs.
 2. It fails: the list opens without taking focus. `]q`{normal} jumps to the
    failing assertion in `inventory_test.go`.
 3. Fix the bug in `Total` (it adds one too many), save, and press
    `<Space>qt`{normal} again. A toast says passed. The list window opens
    only on failure, but one that is already open stays: it now shows the
    passing output, and `<Space>qq`{normal} closes it.
 4. Arguments are passed on: `:Test -run TestRemove`{vim}.
 5. From any other buffer (the quickfix window, a README) `:Test`{vim}
    repeats the last run.

What runs per language:

| Filetype | Command                                                          |
|----------|------------------------------------------------------------------|
| go       | go test -json ./...                                              |
| rust     | cargo test                                                       |
| python   | pytest --tb=short -q (pytest must be on PATH: activate the venv) |

To use a task runner instead, set `vim.g.test_cmd = "just test"`, for one
project in its `.nvim.lua`.

Builds work the same way through Vim's compilers:

 6. `:compiler go`{vim} then `<Space>qm`{normal} (or `:Make`{vim}) runs the
    build in the background. `:compiler cargo`{vim} then `:Make check`{vim}
    in a Rust project. `:MakeStop`{vim} cancels a run.

# Lesson 10: FORMATTING AND LINTING *formatting*

The rule: saving formats a file *only if the project configures the
formatter*. Opening someone else's repository never restyles their code.
`<Space>f`{normal} always formats, config or not.

 1. In `orders.py` look at the `totals = {...}` line in `main`: badly
    spaced. Save with `:w`{vim}. Nothing changes, because the sandbox has no
    Ruff config.
 2. Press `<Space>f`{normal}: formatted. Undo with `u`{normal}.
 3. Now make the project "configure" Ruff: `:e ruff.toml`{vim}, `:w`{vim},
    go back to `orders.py` and save. This time it formats.

What counts as configured:

| Tool               | Marker                                                     |
|--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|
| stylua             | stylua.toml                                                |
| ruff               | ruff.toml, or [tool.ruff] in pyproject.toml                |
| biome / prettier   | their config file; JS and TS use whichever the project has |
| clang-format       | .clang-format                                              |
| rustfmt, goimports | Cargo.toml, go.mod                                         |
| shfmt, taplo       | .editorconfig, taplo.toml                                  |

 4. `<Space>tf`{normal} turns format-on-save off for this buffer,
    `<Space>tF`{normal} everywhere. The statusline shows `fmt off` while it
    is off. Toggle it back.
 5. `<Space>cf`{normal} shows which formatters apply to this buffer and
    why, `<Space>cL`{normal} which linters.

Linters (yamllint, hadolint, clang-tidy) run on save and report through the
same diagnostics as the language servers, so lessons 6 to 8 apply to them.

# Lesson 11: JJ AND REVIEWING CHANGES *changes*

The sandbox is a jj repository. The gutter shows the *current jj change*:
everything you edited since the baseline. The statusline shows the change id
and the nearest bookmark instead of a git hash.

## Hunks

 1. In `orders.py`, `]h`{normal} and `[h`{normal} jump between changed
    hunks.
 2. `<Space>gp`{normal} previews what the hunk replaced.
 3. `<Space>gr`{normal} resets the hunk: your edit is gone, the file is as
    it was. In visual mode it resets only the selected lines.
 4. `ih` is the hunk as a text object: `vih`{normal} selects it,
    `yih`{normal} yanks it.
 5. `<Space>gb`{normal} toggles blame for the current line,
    `<Space>gd`{normal} opens a side-by-side diff of the file.
 6. `<Space>qh`{normal} puts every changed hunk of the repository in the
    quickfix list. `<Space>gS`{normal} lists changed files in a picker.

## Reviewing an agent's work

This is the loop to make a habit of:

 7. Before handing work to an agent, run `jj new` in a shell. The gutter is
    now empty.
 8. When the agent is done, a toast tells you which open buffers were
    reloaded. Everything in the gutter is the agent's work, nothing else.
 9. `<Space>qh`{normal}, then `]q`{normal} through every hunk.
    `<Space>gp`{normal} to see the before, `<Space>gr`{normal} to reject one.

Try it: in the sandbox tab run `:!jj new`{vim} (run from that tab, it acts
on the sandbox), then edit a line and watch only that edit appear.

`<Space>gt`{normal} widens the gutter from the current change to everything
since trunk, to review a whole stack; the statusline shows `base: trunk`.
It needs a repository with a remote, so try it in a real project.

## Splitting a change

jj's interactive commands open Neovim (hunk.nvim) as the diff editor.

10. In a shell (a tmux pane), `cd` into the sandbox, whose path `:pwd`{vim}
    prints in the sandbox tab, and run `jj split`. A file tree and a
    side-by-side diff appear.

| Key         | Does                                  |
|-------------|---------------------------------------|
| a           | toggle the file (tree) or line (diff) |
| A           | toggle the hunk under the cursor      |
| <Tab>       | switch between the two sides          |
| <CR>        | open the file from the tree           |
| <Space><CR> | accept the selection                  |
| q           | abort, nothing changes                |
| g?          | all keys                              |

    The same editor serves `jj squash -i`, `jj commit -i` and
    `jj restore -i`.

In plain git repositories `<Space>gs`{normal} stages a hunk. Under jj there
is nothing to stage, so the key is not mapped there.

# Lesson 12: THE TERMINAL *terminal*

 1. `<M-t>`{normal} (Option-t) opens a floating terminal. The same key hides
    it again, from inside the shell too. The shell keeps running.
 2. `<Esc>`{normal} goes straight to the program in the terminal, with no
    delay, so TUIs behave normally.
 3. `<M-n>`{normal} leaves terminal mode: now the output is a normal buffer.
    Scroll, search with `/`{normal}, yank. `i`{normal} returns to the shell.
 4. Put the cursor on a `file:line` in compiler or test output and press
    `gF`{normal}: that file opens at that line, inside the floating window.
    `<M-t>`{normal} hides the float; the file stays open as a buffer, one
    `<Space><Space>`{normal} away.

Try it: `<M-t>`{normal}, run
`rg -Hn --no-heading Total ~/.cache/nvim/tutor-sandbox/inventory.go`, press
`<M-n>`{normal}, move onto a result and press `gF`{normal}.

`<Space>tt`{normal} also toggles the terminal, from normal mode only.

# Lesson 13: MESSAGES, SESSIONS, TOGGLES *messages*

## Messages and notifications

 1. Run `:map`{vim}. The long output does not stop at "Press ENTER": it
    collapses to a `[+N]` marker. `g<`{normal} opens all of it in a pager
    you can search and yank from.
 2. `<Space>n`{normal} opens the history of every toast shown this session.
    Errors stay on screen for ten seconds, and this is where to reread them.
 3. `:messages`{vim} still holds classic messages and startup errors.

## Sessions

- Starting `nvim` with no arguments restores the session for that
  directory: buffers, windows, tabs. Quitting saves it. With a file
  argument, neither happens.
- `<Space>S`{normal} saves a manual session, `:SessionLoad`{vim} restores it.
- `:restart`{vim} restarts Neovim and the session comes back. Use it after
  changing the config.

## Toggles: everything under `<Space>t`{normal}

| Key       | Toggles                     |
|-----------|-----------------------------|
| <Space>ts | spell check                 |
| <Space>tw | whitespace glyphs           |
| <Space>tW | line wrap                   |
| <Space>th | inlay hints                 |
| <Space>tc | sticky context              |
| <Space>tf | format on save, this buffer |
| <Space>tF | format on save, everywhere  |
| <Space>tt | terminal                    |

## Checks: everything under `<Space>c`{normal}

`<Space>ch`{normal} health of everything, `<Space>cl`{normal} language
servers, `<Space>cf`{normal} formatters, `<Space>cL`{normal} linters.

## Built in, worth remembering

- `:packadd nvim.undotree`{vim} then `:Undotree`{vim}: browse the undo
  history as a tree. Undo history survives restarts here.
- `"`{normal} and `'`{normal} in normal mode, `<C-r>`{normal} in insert
  mode: which-key previews registers and marks before you choose.
- `gx`{normal} opens the URL or path under the cursor.

# Lesson 14: DAILY DRILL AND CHEAT SHEET *drill*

Five minutes, once a day, in the sandbox (`:TutorSandbox!`{vim} first). Do
each line without looking anything up; if you hesitate, that is the one to
repeat tomorrow.

 1. Find `inventory.go` with `<Space>sf`{normal}, jump to `Total` with
    `<Space>/`{normal}.
 2. Run the tests (`<Space>qt`{normal}), jump to the failure
    (`]q`{normal}), fix it, run again.
 3. In `orders.py`: next error (`]d`{normal}), copy it
    (`<Space>yd`{normal}), fix it.
 4. Rename `subtotal` (`grn`{normal}), find its references (`grr`{normal}).
 5. Select a method by structure (`vaf`{normal} or `v`{normal} plus
    `an`{normal}), copy it with context (`<Space>yc`{normal}).
 6. Swap two parameters (`<Space>a`{normal}).
 7. Walk your changes (`]h`{normal}), preview one (`<Space>gp`{normal}),
    reset one (`<Space>gr`{normal}).
 8. All changes to quickfix (`<Space>qh`{normal}), close the list
    (`<Space>qq`{normal}).
 9. Terminal in and out (`<M-t>`{normal}), reopen the last picker
    (`<Space>sr`{normal}).

## Cheat sheet

| Area        | Keys                                    |
|-------------|-----------------------------------------|
| Find        | <Space>sf sg sw / sr s. <Space><Space>  |
| In pickers  | <Tab> alt-a alt-q ctrl-g ctrl-y F1      |
| Files       | - (oil)  ]b [b  <Space>bd  <C-o> <C-i>  |
| Windows     | <C-h> <C-j> <C-k> <C-l>                 |
| Structure   | an in  af ac aa  ]m [m ]] [[  <Space>a  |
| Edit        | sa sd sr  ciq dib  gcc                  |
| LSP         | K gd grr gri grt grn gra gO <Space>ss   |
| Diagnostics | ]d [d <C-w>d <Space>sd <Space>sD        |
| Copy        | <Space>yd yD yw yp yl yc                |
| Quickfix    | ]q [q <Space>qq qd qe qh qt qm :Cfilter |
| Changes     | ]h [h ih <Space>gp gr gb gd gt gS       |
| Format      | <Space>f <Space>tf <Space>tF <Space>cf  |
| Terminal    | <M-t> <M-n> gF                          |
| Messages    | g< <Space>n :messages                   |

Forgot a key? Press `<Space>`{normal} and read. `<Space>sk`{normal} searches
every keymap by its description.

Changing this tutor: it lives in `tutor/config.tutor` in the config. The
checked lines of lesson 1 are listed by line number in
`tutor/config.tutor.json`, so adding or removing lines *above the end of
lesson 1* means updating those numbers.
